---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---
<DocsLayout title="Getting Started | dotfetch">
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <p class="lead">Install dotfetch, register your first tools, retrieve only relevant schemas, and execute chosen tool calls safely.</p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`uv add dotfetch
uv add "dotfetch[openai,lancedb,nomic,mcp,a2a]"
# pip install dotfetch also works`} />

  <h2>1. Import</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`import dotfetch as dot`} />
  <p>The module-level functions work on one shared <code>DotFetch</code>, created on first use. To choose its embedder or vector store, call <code>dot.configure(...)</code> with <code>DotFetch</code>'s arguments before registering anything.</p>

  <h2>2. Register ordinary functions</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`@dot.register(tags=["weather", "demo"])
def get_weather(city: str) -> str:
    """Get the current weather for a city."""
    return f"The weather in {city} is 72°F and sunny."`} />

  <h2>3. Fetch the relevant tools for a prompt</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`messages = [{"role": "user", "content": "What's the weather in San Francisco?"}]
tools = await dot.fetch(messages[0]["content"], limit=3, dialect="openai")

response = await client.chat.completions.create(
    model="gpt-5.5", messages=messages, tools=tools
)`} />

  <div class="callout"><strong>What happens automatically?</strong> dotfetch fingerprints registered tools, syncs them to the configured vector store, retrieves semantically relevant definitions, converts schemas to the requested provider dialect, and leaves execution under your control or the dotfetch execution engine.</div>

  <h2>4. Execute the model's tool calls</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`messages.append(response.choices[0].message)
messages.extend(await dot.execute_tool_calls(response))`} />
  <p>Each call runs through the execution engine (policies, timeouts, retries, rate limits) and comes back formatted for the provider, ready to send on the next turn.</p>

  <h2>Working with instances</h2>
  <p>For more than one registry, or in tests, create instances. Every module-level function above is a method on <code>DotFetch</code>, and <code>dot.default_fetcher()</code> returns the shared one.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`from dotfetch import DotFetch, set_default_fetcher, with_semantic_tools

fetcher = DotFetch()
set_default_fetcher(fetcher)

@fetcher.register(tags=["weather"])
def get_weather(city: str) -> str:
    """Get the current weather for a city."""
    ...

tools = await fetcher.fetch("Is it raining in Leeds?")

# Or let a decorator inject the tools into each call.
@with_semantic_tools(limit=3, dialect="openai")
async def ask_llm(prompt: str, *, tools=None):
    return await client.chat.completions.create(
        model="gpt-5.5",
        messages=[{"role": "user", "content": prompt}],
        tools=tools,
    )`} />

  <h2>Next steps</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    <a class="card" href="/Agent-Gantry/docs/journey/"><h3>Implementation journey</h3><p>Build from local prototype to production runtime.</p></a>
